The best approach is to use the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and already provides `IFileSystem` with mock support. No need to write custom wrappers.

### 1. Install packages

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public bool EnsureDirectory(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

The call sites change minimally — just prefix with `fileSystem.`.

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"val\"}") }
});

var svc = new MyService(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"val\"}", svc.Load("/data/config.json"));

svc.Save("/data/out.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/out.txt"));

Assert.True(svc.EnsureDirectory("/data"));
Assert.False(svc.EnsureDirectory("/nope"));
```

**Key points:**
- Only wrap the methods you actually call (`ReadAllText`, `WriteAllText`, `Directory.Exists`) — don't over-abstract.
- Register as `AddSingleton` since `FileSystem` is stateless.
- `MockFileSystem` provides an in-memory filesystem — no temp files needed in tests.

